WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:06.999
Before a chip goes to the foundry, its block diagram is the most complete record of what will be built.

2
00:00:07.549 --> 00:00:20.552
This film walks DG32-LITE through that record: clocking and reset, the lockstep cores, memory, the bus and its peripherals, the pads, production test, and the checks that stand before tape-in.

3
00:00:21.102 --> 00:00:26.980
Everything here describes the intended die; nothing has yet been measured on silicon.

4
00:00:28.180 --> 00:00:29.304
The short version.

5
00:00:29.854 --> 00:00:37.759
One fifty megahertz clock domain, with the lockstep core measured at fifty-five to sixty-two megahertz after place-and-route.

6
00:00:38.309 --> 00:00:43.684
A checker that runs two cycles behind and sees the main core's bus responses.

7
00:00:44.234 --> 00:00:47.531
Forty-four wrapper pads, every one of them allocated.

8
00:00:48.081 --> 00:00:52.044
And four sign-off gates that must all pass on the final die.

9
00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:55.600
Timing starts simple on purpose.

10
00:00:56.150 --> 00:01:02.665
A single fifty megahertz input clocks everything, so there is no crossing between domains to verify.

11
00:01:03.215 --> 00:01:12.404
Software can shut off the clock to eight blocks it is not using, from the serial ports to the CORDIC, and in test mode all eight are forced on.

12
00:01:12.954 --> 00:01:21.905
Reset is asynchronous, a supervisor cleans up the supply-good signals, and the checker core leaves reset two cycles after the main core.

13
00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:30.396
The hard part of lockstep is not comparing two cores; it is keeping them from disagreeing when nothing is wrong.

14
00:01:30.946 --> 00:01:37.965
Interrupts and peripheral reads are the usual culprits, because the second core would see them at a different moment.

15
00:01:38.515 --> 00:01:45.356
Here the checker gets a delayed copy of everything the main core saw, so normal code cannot split them.

16
00:01:45.906 --> 00:01:51.551
That behaviour has been checked in simulation, and a real mismatch trips the fault pin.

17
00:01:52.750 --> 00:01:55.377
Instructions never compete with data.

18
00:01:55.927 --> 00:02:06.013
Each SRAM block has two ports: the processor fetches code through one while data moves through the other, so data traffic cannot stall execution.

19
00:02:06.563 --> 00:02:13.957
A fetch aimed anywhere but the ROM or that code port raises a fault instead of running whatever it finds.

20
00:02:14.507 --> 00:02:25.285
The ROM is hard-wired logic that checks the flash image, copies it into SRAM and hands over, or opens a serial monitor if the flash is blank.

21
00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:29.987
The bus is built so that no access can hang the chip.

22
00:02:30.537 --> 00:02:36.951
Only two things can drive it, the processor and the DMA, and the processor wins any tie.

23
00:02:37.501 --> 00:02:42.918
Once a transfer starts, its destination is locked until the reply comes back.

24
00:02:43.468 --> 00:02:52.068
If software touches an address with nothing behind it, or a peripheral whose clock is switched off, it gets an error instead of a hang.

25
00:02:52.618 --> 00:02:57.734
Flash is visible only as data; code always runs from SRAM.

26
00:02:58.930 --> 00:03:02.458
Eighteen blocks share that bus, in three groups of six.

27
00:03:03.008 --> 00:03:11.564
Six connect the chip to the outside world: the UARTs, SPI, I squared C, the QSPI window, GPIO and the timers.

28
00:03:12.114 --> 00:03:19.239
Six serve the motor: PWM, DShot, encoder and Hall, CORDIC, the ADC and interrupts.

29
00:03:19.789 --> 00:03:29.887
The last six are there so the chip can be trusted and tested: system control, the watchdog, the fault register, the error slave, scan and JTAG.

30
00:03:31.090 --> 00:03:34.966
Every one of the forty-four wrapper pads has a job.

31
00:03:35.516 --> 00:03:43.996
Seven carry the QSPI flash, seven the PWM outputs and trigger, and six the encoder and Hall inputs.

32
00:03:44.546 --> 00:03:48.187
Four each go to the UARTs, SPI and JTAG.

33
00:03:48.737 --> 00:03:50.496
Three are GPIO.

34
00:03:51.046 --> 00:04:00.279
Two each carry I squared C, supply-good and the analog ADC inputs, and one each carry the clock, the reset and the fault output.

35
00:04:01.480 --> 00:04:08.762
Every manufactured part needs a production test, and the JTAG port is how DG32-LITE gets one.

36
00:04:09.312 --> 00:04:15.528
A tester switches the chip into scan mode and shifts patterns through thirteen chains of flip-flops.

37
00:04:16.078 --> 00:04:21.851
Because test mode turns every gated clock back on, no block is hidden from those patterns.

38
00:04:22.401 --> 00:04:28.895
The same pins do not offer interactive CPU debugging yet; that is a later roadmap item.

39
00:04:30.090 --> 00:04:32.315
Tape-in waits on four checks.

40
00:04:32.865 --> 00:04:36.087
The layout must obey every manufacturing rule.

41
00:04:36.637 --> 00:04:41.322
It must describe exactly the circuit that was designed, down to its pins.

42
00:04:41.872 --> 00:04:44.765
Every pad must be wired through to logic.

43
00:04:45.315 --> 00:04:50.271
And the routed netlist, simulated gate by gate, must boot and print its banner.

44
00:04:50.821 --> 00:04:56.955
The third check is there because a layout can clear the first two and still leave a pad unconnected.

45
00:04:57.505 --> 00:05:01.443
What this film states is the bar, not whether it has been cleared.

46
00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:05.915
If you keep three points from this, make them these.

47
00:05:06.465 --> 00:05:11.355
The die has one clock, so its timing has no domain crossings to go wrong.

48
00:05:11.905 --> 00:05:15.861
No access can hang it, and no pad is left without a job.

49
00:05:16.411 --> 00:05:20.515
And nothing goes to the foundry until four checks all pass.

50
00:05:21.065 --> 00:05:29.049
What comes next is first silicon, via the September twenty twenty-six shuttle, where these design records meet measurement.

